 [], "synonyms": [], "antonyms": [], "translations": [], "subsenses": []}, {"definition": "Alternative letter-case form of Beaver (“member of the youngest wing of the Scout movement”).", "tags": ["alt of", "countable", "uncountable"], "examples": [], "quotes": [], "synonyms": [], "antonyms": [], "translations": [], "subsenses": []}, {"definition": "(countable, slang) A beard or a bearded person.", "tags": ["countable", "slang"], "examples": [], "quotes": [{"text": "The beards were false ones. I could see the elastic going over their ears. In other words, I had fallen among a band of criminals who were not wilful beavers, but had merely assumed the fungus for purposes of disguise.", "reference": "1936, P. G. Wodehouse, Laughing Gas:"}], "synonyms": ["beard", "beardo", "beardy"], "antonyms": [], "translations": [], "subsenses": []}, {"definition": "(uncountable, historical, slang) A game, in which points are scored by spotting beards.", "tags": ["historical", "slang", "uncountable"], "examples": [], "quotes": [], "synonyms": [], "antonyms": [], "translations": [], "subsenses": []}, {"definition": "(chiefly Canada, US, slang, countable) The pubic hair near a vulva or a vulva itself; (attributively) denoting films or literature featuring nude women.", "tags": ["Canada", "US", "countable", "slang"], "examples": [], "quotes": [{"text": "Finally it came on. It was a beauty, a beaver flick made in the late 1970s. It was called Big Black Leather Splits.", "reference": "1969, Harlan Ellison, “A Boy and His Dog”, in The Beast that Shouted Love at the Heart of the World, New York: Open Road Media, published 2014, →ISBN:"}, {"text": "Between you and me, uh, she might have been fifteen, but when you get that little red beaver right up there in front of you, I don’t think it's crazy at all and I don’t think you do either.", "reference": "1975, Lawrence Hauben, Bo Goldman, directed by Miloš Forman, One Flew over the Cuckoo’s Nest (motion picture), spoken by Randle McMurphy (Jack Nicholson):"}, {"text": "Let’s get some of that Saturday night beaver.", "reference": "1997, Paul Thomas Anderson, Boogie Nights (motion picture), spoken by Reed Rothchild (John C. Reilly):"}, {"text": "The store sold beaver books, fuck-suck books, homo books, novels, films, slides, playing cards, dildoes, cock rings, S&M gear, and French ticklers.", "reference": "2004, James Ellroy, Destination: Morgue! L.A. Tales, New York: Vintage Books, →ISBN, pages 127–128:"}, {"text": "[…] once she wore none at all, swears to this day that he saw her beaver that fateful Friday night.", "reference": "2010, Dennis McFadden, Hart’s Grove: Stories:"}], "synonyms": ["beav", "nest"], "antonyms": [], "translations": [], "subsenses": []}, {"definition": "(US, offensive, slang) A woman, especially one who is sexually attractive.", "tags": ["US", "countable", "offensive", "slang", "uncountable"], "examples": [], "quotes": [{"text": "“10-4, Beaver [CB talk for a female], we’re all going down to Plains tomorrow after Jimmy Carter wins.”", "reference": "1977 January 13, Dan Aykroyd, John Belushi, “Jimmy Carter: New South Burn”, in Rolling Stone, [San Francisco or New York], retrieved 01 Dec 2022:"}], "synonyms": [], "antonyms": [], "translations": [], "subsenses": []}], "synonyms": [], "antonyms": []}, {"language": {"code": "en", "name": "English"}, "partOfSpeech": "verb", "pronunciations": [{"type": "ipa", "text": "/ˈbiːvə/", "tags": ["Received Pronunciation"]}, {"type": "ipa", "text": "/ˈbivɚ/", "tags": ["General American"]}], "forms": [{"word": "beavers", "tags": ["present", "singular", "third-person"]}, {"word": "beavering", "tags": ["participle", "present"]}, {"word": "beavered", "tags": ["participle", "past"]}, {"word": "beavered", "tags": ["past"]}], "senses": [{"definition": "To form a felt-like texture, similar to the way beaver fur is used for felt-making.", "tags": [], "examples": [], "quotes": [{"text": "Without these attentions the woad will not beaver well, a term descriptive of the fineness of the capillary filaments into which it draws out when broken between the finger and thumb.", "reference": "1799, Arthur Young, General View of the Agriculture of the County of Lincoln, London, page 155:"}], "synonyms": [], "antonyms": [], "translations": [], "subsenses": []}, {"definition": "To work hard.", "tags": [], "examples": [], "quotes": [{"text": "When A. G. Dickens published his English Reformation in 1964 the archival beavering of a generation of graduate students was given its imprimatur in the claim to understand how the English people felt about religious change—largely, according to Dickens, positively.", "reference": "2017, Felicity Heal, “Changing Interpretations of the British Reformations”, in Ulinka Rublack, editor, The Oxford Handbook of the Protestant Reformations, Oxford: Oxford University Press, →ISBN, page 234:"}], "synonyms": [], "antonyms": [], "translations": [], "subsenses": []}, {"definition": "(logging, slang) To cut a continuous ring around a tree that one is felling.", "tags": ["slang"], "examples": [], "quotes": [], "synonyms": [], "antonyms": [], "translations": [], "subsenses": []}, {"definition": "(backgammon) After being doubled, to immediately double the stakes again, a move that keeps the doubling cube on one’s own side of the board.", "tags": [], "examples": [], "quotes": [], "synonyms": [], "antonyms": [], "translations": [], "subsenses": []}, {"definition": "(slang) To spot a beard in a game of beaver.", "tags": ["slang"], "examples": [], "quotes": [{"text": "Beavering of foreign visitors does not count. This is a rule, but it is never carried out.", "reference": "1922 October 13, Roanoke World-News, Roanoke, Virginia, retrieved 02 Dec 2022, page 6:"}], "synonyms": [], "antonyms": [], "translations": [], "subsenses": []}], "synonyms": [], "antonyms": []}, {"language": {"code": "en", "name": "English"}, "partOfSpeech": "noun", "pronunciations": [{"type": "ipa", "text": "/ˈbiːvə/", "tags": ["Received Pronunciation"]}, {"type": "ipa", "text": "/ˈbivɚ/", "tags": ["General American"]}], "forms": [{"word": "beavers", "tags": ["plural"]}], "senses": [{"definition": "Alternative spelling of bevor (“part of a helmet”).", "tags": ["alt of", "alternative"], "examples": ["With trembling hands her beaver he untied, / Which done, he saw, and seeing knew her face.", "1951 Adaptation of the 1885 Ormsby translation of Cervantes’ Don Quixote, correcting Ormsby as to the portion of the helmet referred to by Cervantes (see note 11 to chapter II) at the suggestion of Juan Hartzenbusch, a 19th-century director of the National Library of Spain.\nThey laid a table for him at the door of the inn for the sake of the air, and the host brought him a portion of ill-soaked and worse cooked stockfish, and a piece of bread as black and mouldy as his own armour; but a laughble sight it was to see him eating, for having his helmet on and the beaver up, he could not with his own hands put anything into his mouth unless some one else placed it there, and this service one of the ladies rendered him."], "quotes": [{"text": "Lord Stafford’s father, Duke of Buckingham,\nIs either slain or wounded dangerously;\nI cleft his beaver with a downright blow:", "reference": "c. 1591–1592 (date written), William Shakespeare, “The Third Part of Henry the Sixt, […]”, in Mr. William Shakespeares Comedies, Histories, & Tragedies […] (First Folio), London: […] Isaac Iaggard, and Ed[ward] Blount, published 1623, →OCLC, [Act I, scene i]:"}, {"text": "Without alighting from his horse, the conqueror called for a bowl of wine, and opening the beaver, or lower part of his helmet, announced that he quaffed it, “To all true English hearts, and to the confusion of foreign tyrants.”", "reference": "1819, Walter Scott, Ivanhoe:"}, {"text": "As each one brings a little of himself to what he sees you brought the trappings of your historic preoccupations, so that Monsieur flattered you by presenting himself with beaver up like Hamlet's father's ghost!", "reference": "1974, Lawrence Durrell, Monsieur, or the Prince of Darkness, Faber & Faber, published 1992, page 128:"}], "synonyms": [], "antonyms": [], "translations": [], "subsenses": []}], "synonyms": [], "antonyms": []}, {"language": {"code": "en", "name": "English"}, "partOfSpeech": "noun", "pronunciations": [{"type": "ipa", "text": "/ˈbiːvə/", "tags": ["Received Pronunciation"]}, {"type": "ipa", "text": "/ˈbivɚ/", "tags": ["General American"]}], "forms": [], "senses": [{"definition": "Butter.", "tags": [], "examples": [], "quotes": [{"text": "Butter – Beaver.", "reference": "[1754, The Scoundrel’s Dictionary, London, page 15:"}], "synonyms": [], "antonyms": [], "translations": [], "subsenses": []}], "synonyms": [], "antonyms": []}], "source": {"url": "https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/beaver", "license": {"name": "CC BY-SA 4.0", "url": "https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by-sa/4.0/"}}}}